ARTICLES=[
 {'slug':'brand-strategy-before-channels','title':'Brand strategy before channels: a practical starting point','seo':'Brand Strategy Before Channels: A Practical Guide | PRIMO Hub','description':'A practical guide to defining your audience, offer and message before choosing marketing channels, campaigns or a content schedule.','topic':'strategy','image':'photo-1552664730-d307ca884978.jpg','intro':'Before deciding how often to post or which platform to advertise on, make a few choices about who you want to reach and why they should care.','sections':[
 ('Start with the decision you want to influence','A marketing brief often begins with a channel: more Instagram content, a Google campaign or a new website. Those can be useful tools, but the first question is what needs to change for the business. Do people need to discover the brand, understand a new offer, compare services or return more often? Choose one main decision to influence. A clear objective makes it easier to judge which activity belongs in the plan and which can wait.'),
 ('Describe the audience in a useful way','An audience description should help the team make creative decisions. Age and location may matter, but the situation matters too. A parent looking for learning support has different questions from a visitor choosing a restaurant for a special occasion. Write down what the person is trying to achieve, what may hold them back and what information would help. This gives a designer, writer or media planner something more useful than a broad demographic label.'),
 ('Make the offer easy to explain','Try explaining the offer without using a slogan. State what it is, who it helps and the next step. If the explanation changes depending on who is speaking, the marketing may need a clearer message before it needs more reach. This does not remove personality. It gives the personality a job: making a useful, distinctive offer easier to recognise and remember.'),
 ('Choose proof you can actually support','A strong message needs a reason to believe it. That might be a real product detail, an approved customer story, a clear explanation of the process or a useful demonstration. Avoid building the campaign around claims the team cannot substantiate. Decide which evidence is available now and which would need approval. This makes content production more focused and reduces the gap between what the campaign promises and what the customer experiences.'),
 ('Let the channel follow the job','Once the audience, offer and next action are clear, compare the available channels. Search can be useful when people already know what they need. Social content can help introduce a perspective or demonstrate an experience. A website can organise the details that support a decision. The best combination depends on the journey, the available creative and the team’s ability to maintain it. A smaller, coherent plan is often easier to learn from than a presence everywhere.'),
 ('Turn the strategy into a working brief','Finish with a one-page brief: the business objective, audience situation, main message, available proof, intended action and how the team will review progress. Use it to check the first round of content and campaign ideas. If an idea does not help the audience take the intended next step, refine it or leave it out. Strategy becomes useful when it changes what the team makes next.')],
 'related':'digital-marketing'},
 {'slug':'website-conversion-checklist','title':'A clearer website journey: from first visit to enquiry','seo':'Website Enquiry Journey Checklist | PRIMO Hub','description':'Review your website journey with practical checks for page purpose, navigation, content, mobile contact and meaningful measurement.','topic':'websites','image':'photo-1499951360447-b19be8fe80f5.jpg','intro':'Before adding more traffic, check whether the website gives a visitor enough information and a clear way to take the next step.','sections':[
 ('Give the page one primary purpose','A visitor should be able to understand why a page exists. A service page might explain an offer and support an enquiry. A restaurant page might help someone choose a menu and find booking information. A campaign landing page might focus on one programme or promotion. Write down the page’s primary purpose, then look for competing messages that make it harder to understand. Secondary actions can remain, but they should not overwhelm the decision the page is meant to support.'),
 ('Answer the questions in the right order','Look at the page as someone who is not already familiar with the business. Can they tell what is offered, who it is for and what makes it relevant? Are practical details available where they need them? Long pages are not automatically a problem; unclear sequence is. Use descriptive headings and group related information. The visitor should be able to scan the structure and then choose where to read more.'),
 ('Make navigation predictable','Navigation labels should describe the destination. A visitor looking for services or contact details should not need to decode a creative label before finding them. Check the main menu, footer and links inside the content. Important pages should be connected by ordinary links that work without an animation or special gesture. On mobile, review how the menu opens, how it closes and whether the main action remains easy to reach.'),
 ('Reduce friction in the enquiry step','Ask for the information the team needs to begin a useful conversation. Explain what happens when the visitor submits the form, and make errors easy to understand. If a button opens an email application or a separate booking tool, label that action accurately. Test the journey on a phone using the same connection and screen size a customer might use. A clear form is part of the brand experience, not just a technical detail.'),
 ('Use imagery and motion with a purpose','Photography can help people understand the atmosphere of a venue or the character of a brand. Motion can guide attention or show a relationship between elements. Neither should hide the main information or make the next action difficult to use. Give images useful alternative text where appropriate, keep text readable over photography and respect reduced-motion preferences. The experience should still make sense when visual effects are reduced.'),
 ('Review actions, not just visits','Traffic is useful context, but it does not explain the whole journey. Decide which actions indicate meaningful progress: opening service details, beginning an enquiry or completing a booking step. Check that measurement matches the actual behaviour and that the team understands its limitations. Then make one focused improvement and review what changes. This creates a practical cycle of observation, design and learning.')],
 'related':'website-development'},
 {'slug':'marketing-reporting-that-leads-to-action','title':'Marketing reporting that leads to a useful next action','seo':'How to Build Useful Marketing Reports | PRIMO Hub','description':'A practical guide to connecting marketing reports with business questions, meaningful measures, attribution context and a clear next action.','topic':'insights','image':'photo-1519389950473-47ba0277781c.jpg','intro':'A useful report should help the team make a decision. Start with the question the business needs to answer, then choose the measures.','sections':[
 ('Begin with the business question','A dashboard can contain a great deal of information without helping anyone decide what to do. Start the reporting conversation with a question. Are the campaigns attracting the intended audience? Is the website helping interested visitors enquire? Which offer deserves another creative test? A clear question gives the report a purpose and helps the team distinguish essential information from background detail.'),
 ('Choose a small set of connected measures','Different measures describe different parts of the journey. Reach can help explain distribution. Website visits can show that people followed the message. Enquiries may indicate a stronger level of intent. Keep those roles separate rather than presenting every increase as the same kind of success. Choose a short set of measures linked to the agreed objective, and explain where each one comes from.'),
 ('Keep the context beside the numbers','A result becomes more useful when the team understands what changed. Add context such as budget, campaign timing, creative updates, landing-page changes and seasonal activity. A comparison between two periods is harder to interpret when those conditions are different. The report should make that visible. Context does not excuse a result; it helps the team ask a better question about it.'),
 ('Be clear about attribution','Platforms and analytics tools may describe the same customer journey differently. A person might discover a brand through content, search for it later and enquire after another visit. Avoid adding platform-reported conversions together without understanding the definitions. Explain which measurement source is being used and what it can tell you. This makes discussion more reliable and reduces the temptation to present certainty that the data does not support.'),
 ('Turn the review into a short action list','For each main finding, record the next action, the reason for it and who owns it. A weak message may call for new creative. A high level of irrelevant traffic may call for a targeting review. A useful audience with few enquiries may call for a closer look at the destination page. The action should follow the finding rather than defaulting to more spend or more content.'),
 ('Use the next report to close the loop','The following review should revisit the previous action list. What was changed, what happened and what remains uncertain? This builds continuity between reports and makes experimentation easier to follow. Over time, the report becomes a record of learning rather than a collection of disconnected monthly slides. That is a more useful foundation for planning the next stage of marketing work.')],
 'related':'digital-marketing'}
]
